WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.680
This is not going to be a normal video.

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:05.900
We're sending garlic bread
to the edge of space!

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.900
Loads of people have launched
loads of objects on balloons,

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.200
usually as publicity stunts.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.750
But despite a lot of
breathless press releases

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.420
those objects haven't
been launched into space.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.630
Most standards organisations agree
that space officially starts

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.110
at the completely arbitrary
Kármán Line, 100 kilometres up.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:34.720
And balloons like that one
only get about a third of the way there.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.510
And we're definitely not
sending anything into orbit.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:42.020
Orbit requires tens of thousands of
miles-an-hour of speed.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:43.820
That's what all the rockets are for.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.500
- We're using a weather balloon.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.560
It's a large one, actually, today.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.420
We're going as high as we can
with this payload.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:53.540
It's very cold on the way up.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:56.960
It gets warmer as you approach
that sort of altitude.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.650
So it's coldest just above the jet stream

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.580
and then getting slightly warmer again.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:06.690
It gets back down to about zero
about where the balloon will pop.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:10.220
- The idea that all those publicity stunts
actually made it to space

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:14.440
is helped by the fact that the fish-eye lens
on some of the cameras that they use

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:18.130
means that the curve of the earth
looks a lot more dramatic

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:19.910
than it really is at that height.

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:21.940
Now we're using cameras that
correct for that,

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:26.290
so what you'll see on screen
is more or less what you'd see up there.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:30.530
So we're not saying "space",
we're saying "the edge of space".

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:33.960
Which is basically just a marketing term,
but the atmosphere's so thin up there,

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.930
about 1% of the pressure at ground level,
that it's close enough.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.470
Why garlic bread?

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:41.140
Because it's delicious,

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:45.400
and because someone already sent pizza
up in a balloon a few years ago.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.610
- This garlic bread is delicious.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.680
It's homemade.
Well, apart from the baguette.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.310
I did some homemade garlic butter on there
with some real nice Parmesan on it.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.440
Although I did make it at
5 o'clock this morning!

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:57.930
So it's going to be in near-vacuum.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.760
It's going to be possibly frozen. I mean...

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.950
We're going to send half up
in the sky with the balloon

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:05.810
and then leave half on earth
for a real comparison taste test.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.660
- As it goes up, the atmosphere is getting
thinner and thinner

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.560
and there's less and less air pushing in.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.140
The balloon itself will
get bigger and bigger.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.200
So eventually the balloon will pop

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.250
and the equipment will
parachute down to the ground

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.354
and we'll go and recover it.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:42.720
We normally predict the landing spot
to within about five miles

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.563
when we launch the balloon.

00:02:44.563 --> 00:02:48.180
We're tracking the balloon using some radio trackers.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.620
They send a signal with a
GPS position to the ground

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:56.860
and that's put on a map and we chase
the balloon's predicted landing spot.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.410
I've done lots of
high-altitude ballooning.

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:01.810
I've been doing it now for about 10 years.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:05.560
Never lost one. Sent one to
its doom a couple of times.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.103
- All sorts of food items have been
launched into the stratosphere.

00:03:08.103 --> 00:03:10.670
The BBC sent wedding cake up
as part of a children's show

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.970
but the punchline at the end was
that no one actually ate it.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:17.700
I've yet to find any balloon-launched food
that was actually eaten after landing.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.809
And the main reason for that is
you have no idea

00:03:19.809 --> 00:03:21.970
where it's going to land,
what it's going land in,

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:25.410
or what animals will have got to it first.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:29.310
- The box I've designed has a GPS
and a little servo,

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.830
and a piece of string and some springs.

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:36.683
As it comes down it closes
the servo 1000 metres above the ground.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.963
- So now we have to get in our cars
and go chasing the payload.

00:03:46.962 --> 00:03:50.000
It's just coming in to land somewhere
about half a mile ahead

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.960
so, Barry, keep your eyes on the sky.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:53.610
I've got to keep my eyes on the road.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:57.890
You reckon we're okay to park here?

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:00.213
Let's go.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.320
- It's just on the end of that row there.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:09.420
- Let's do it.

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:12.860
- Oh dear!
- I think this is the way.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:16.580
Well, at least we've got
two cameras, I suppose.

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:21.783
Yay, I think they're still running.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:25.424
- Oh,there we go.
- Lift it up, oh.

00:04:25.424 --> 00:04:26.803
- Oh, yes!
- Yeah.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:29.620
- Alright so this is your original?

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:31.160
- Yeah, yeah.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:32.230
- That's really good.

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:33.750
- Not bad, right?

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:34.793
- Space bread...

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:36.760
- Is it cold?

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.320
- It's not that much... oh!

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:41.680
No, that tore completely differently.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:44.900
So that one ripped;
that one went [tearing sound].

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:46.720
This one went click.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:48.360
- It's definitely got an icy middle.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:49.233
- Oh wow.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.460
- I don't know. I mean...
- That has been frozen.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:54.350
That's been frozen in the stratosphere.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:56.654
- You can sorta see the colour
of the middle of them.

00:04:56.654 --> 00:04:57.580
It's whiter, isn't it?

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:00.080
- This went to the stratosphere
and I'm eating it!

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:02.860
Sort of.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:06.360
Thank you very much to Steve Randall
from Random Aerospace

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:09.040
and to Barry Lewis from My Virgin Kitchen.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:11.610
I don't actually know what I did on this.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:13.600
I'm basically DJ Khaled at this point.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.360
- Yeah! Yeah.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.570
DJ Garl-ed? No, it didn't work.

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:18.970
I was gonna say garlic bread.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:20.170
- We're done, we're good.
- Yes. Sorry.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.033
Thank you, folks!

